  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Mud crab aquaculture</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The mud crab farming project will be implemented by a joint venture encompassing four partners:&#x2022; R&#xe9;union P&#xe9;lagique from Reunion Island, France, as the applicant.&#x2022; Copefrito, from Toliara, Madagascar as the local partner.&#x2022; Sogediproma from Mahajanga, Madagascar as the third partner.&#x2022; Sephal from Antananarivo, Madagascar as the fourth partner.Globally, around 205,000 tons of mud crab had been produced in 2012, among them, 160,000 came from aquaculture. The high demand for this product has led to overexploitation in many countries, which now suffer from resource depletion.In Madagascar, there are three main production zones of mud crab: the Boina region (around Mahajanga), the Menabe region (around Morondava) and the Sofia region (around Maromandia). As mud crabs live in mangrove, where industrial boats cant navigate, fishery is exclusively traditional.Since aquaculture doesnt exist yet in Madagascar, collecting is therefore the only way to produce mud crabs in the country which is already resulting in overexploitation. At the same time, area of the natural habitat of the mud crab, the mangrove forests, are rapidly reducing.To meet the large demand for mud crab in Asia, and to protect the countrys resources, aquaculture, including reproduction of crabs is the natural and unique long-term viable solution. The project will implement two distinct production chains: soft shell crabs*, implying a reproduction process, and alive crabs, based on catch. About 25% of the projects fattened mud crab output will be generated through outreach activities with the surrounding villages where fishermen will be supported to set up their own mud crab fattening farms. There will be two main farms, in Mahajanga and in Toliara, and an exportation site in Antananarivo where the live crabs are being prepared for transport to mainly Asia.* Soft shell crabs are small crabs which can be eaten whole. They are mostly used for crab cakes conception. The principle is to pick up and freeze young crabs just after mottling, before their new shell has hardened.</narrative>
  </description>
